# Files that are EXECUTED ON LINUX must keep LF endings, regardless of the
# checkout platform.
#
# Development happens on Windows with core.autocrlf=true, so without this the
# working copy of a shell script gets CRLF. Copying that to the production box
# (docs/deploying-production.md tells you to `install` it there) yields a script
# bash cannot run: the carriage return becomes part of the shebang and of every
# command, producing errors like
#     /usr/bin/env: 'bash\r': No such file or directory
#     $'\r': command not found
# which say nothing about the actual cause.
#
# Deliberately narrow. `* text=auto eol=lf` would rewrite every file in the
# working tree and is not wanted on a Windows machine - only the files Linux has
# to execute are pinned.
*.sh text eol=lf

# The systemd unit and the nginx snippets quoted in the runbook are read by
# Linux daemons; CRLF in a unit file is a parse error.
*.service text eol=lf
